## Reviewer got wrong (not applied, or applied only in the corrected form)

- ur-a 1 and ur-a 3. The English does say courts generally impose those terms, and that the attorney should pursue the maximum available. Those are the sentences RULEBOOK R3 deliberately softened, and ACCEPTED-ROUND forbids making a result sentence positive. Restoring “عام طور پر” and “زیادہ سے زیادہ معاوضہ” would undo that. Left in the R3 wording.
- ur-a 7. The quote “بنیاد بنت۔” drops ے (U+06D2). The file already had “بنیاد بنتے۔”, which is the verb بنتے plus the Urdu full stop, not the noun بنت (“daughter”). The finite auxiliary ہیں was still missing, so the suggested “بنتے ہیں” was applied. The diagnosis “daughter, verb missing” is wrong; the missing piece was ہیں only.
- ur-a 10 and 11. Pakistani lakh grouping and spelling out 1.57 million are reasonable for a Pakistan reader. The checker does not parse lakh grouping, and a test pins the bytes `TWD 1.57M`. Not applied.
- ur-b 12. The zero-width spaces are real, and a line that contains only U+200B looks blank. They are not stray Urdu defects: the Korean source has the same counts, and the checker fails if the counts diverge. Not deleted.
- ur-b 16, “also the opening of that section”. The section 3 body already opened with “جوڑے کی باقی ماندہ جائیداد”, where کی agrees with جائیداد. Only the FAQ question and the heading had “شریک حیات کا”. The opening was left.
